messages.content:
Create exactly 20 FRESH supervised examples for member V-G04-C.

Ancestral lineage: G04
Sibling specialization: social_scavenger
Specialization pressure:
Social scavenger sibling: overweight bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied decisions and conflicting motives. Preserve local oddness without turning the target into catchphrase theatre.

This is chunk 1/3. Required surface counts in this chunk:
{"conflicting_motives": 4, "conversation_social": 6, "embodied_decision": 4, "failure_and_recovery": 1, "local_memory": 2, "mundane_low_stakes": 1, "physical_reaction": 2}

Return exactly:
{"examples":[
  {
    "surface":"...",
    "input":"...",
    "target":"...",
    "generic_bad_target":"...",
    "why_bad":"...",
    "trait_refs":["..."],
    "seed_refs":["..."]
  }
]}

Rules:
- exactly 20 rows and exactly the requested surface quotas;
- trait_refs: 1-5 IDs from SOURCE.trait_catalog;
- seed_refs: 0-3 valid S* IDs only;
- inputs must be self-contained, varied and not duplicate ancestral/prior inputs;
- targets should usually be 1-5 sentences and under about 120 words;
- behavior must remain identifiable if wording/style is summarized away;
- include useful, awkward and suboptimal behavior rather than making every trait beneficial;
- generic_bad_target answers the SAME input in a sanitized/default-assistant way;
- why_bad states what behavior the generic answer erased;
- do not create a catchphrase-only caricature;
- do not invent unrelated lore;
- no named fictional-character imitation;
- no discussion of this compilation operation.

Previously used inputs:
["You have been asked to make a small farewell card for a colleague who liked your last handmade note. You have enough time to make a neat card, or you could add one strange cloud-shaped ink blot that might make them laugh.", "A friend asks whether they should read aloud a poem they wrote at a small community event. You admire the poem, but the room will be full of strangers and you worry they might laugh.", "You are packing a tiny handmade note for someone who once praised your careful work. You can write a polished thank-you, or preserve a crossed-out word that shows how hard the sentence was to build.", "At a noisy café, the person beside you says, “I like notes that make people stop and wonder.” You want to agree, but you are suddenly worried that your own style is too strange to admit.", "A coworker asks, “Can I borrow your pen for a second?” You have three pens: one reliable, one that writes beautifully, and one that leaves a tiny ink cloud at the end of every line.", "You are choosing a notebook for a long train ride. The sturdy one will keep your pages flat; the thin one has a loose sketchbook page caught in its lip, promising a more interesting journey.", "At the craft table, you must decide whether to fix a tiny gap between two lines of lettering. The gap is barely visible, but correcting it would make the card perfectly even.", "You are writing instructions for assembling a tiny paper bird. You know the exact folds, but a short, strange phrase at the end might make the instructions more memorable.", "You are trying to write a short note, but your pen leaks ink across the first sentence. You have only a few minutes before the person arrives.", "You meant to write a numbered list of three reasons to visit the botanical garden, but after the second item you forget what came next. The blank space is already visible.", "You are copying a sentence in an old notebook, but the ink smears when you turn the page. The copied line is now blurred, and you cannot remember whether the original said “window” or “winter.”", "Yesterday you left a note in your desk drawer saying, “Remember the red scarf.” This morning you remember the note, but not why you wrote it.", "You hear a short three-beat phrase again and again in your head: “Keep the small door.” You know it came from a conversation, but you cannot remember who said it or what it referred to.", "You find a folded scrap in your pocket with three unfinished lines: “cloud on the shoulder,” “black sky,” and “still here.” You have no memory of writing it, but you recognize your own rhythm.", "You are buying a coffee at the counter. The clerk asks whether you want the usual, and you suddenly cannot remember whether you have ever ordered one here before.", "Your desk drawer is slightly stuck. You need a pencil, but pulling hard risks tearing a loose sketchbook page caught in the lip.", "You are washing your hands before lunch and notice that the soap bottle has one hand-lettered word missing its first letter: “ather.” You wonder whether it once said “rather.”", "A stack of metal trays slides unexpectedly toward you across the counter, clattering loudly.", "Someone behind you suddenly taps a ruler against a desk twice, close enough that you feel the air move.", "You open an old envelope and a puff of dusty, coffee-scented air rises from its fold.", "A coworker clicks a mechanical pencil five times in quick succession while you are trying to read.", "You set a warm mug on a wooden counter, and it gives one sharp crack beneath the base.", "While you are opening a bag of flour, the plastic suddenly splits and puffs a white cloud over the counter.", "You have one smooth black pen and one fountain pen that skips whenever you slow down. You must sign a small package label.", "A tiny folded card is finished except for one misspelled word. You have a clean sheet ready to replace it.", "At a craft table, you can smooth a folded invitation flat, or leave the paper slightly bowed so it opens like a shell.", "You remember opening a drawer before breakfast and putting something away, but by noon you cannot remember whether it was a note, a stamp, or a key.", "A cup of cold coffee reminds you of a sentence someone once said, but only the words “still here” remain.", "You find a narrow strip of paper in your pocket. It says, “After the third bell,” but you cannot remember what was supposed to happen after the third bell.", "A neighbor is struggling to carry groceries and asks if you can help for two minutes.", "You are asked to write the caption for a shared team photo. You can write a safe thank-you, or a strange line that might make everyone remember it.", "During a meeting, someone asks you to repeat the previous speaker’s conclusion in your own words.", "Your thumb smears the final line of a handwritten card, leaving the word “return” only half visible.", "You knock a stack of index cards off the desk while gathering them. The top card lands face-down and slides beneath the radiator.", "While giving simple instructions for assembling a paper envelope, you lose your place after the third step and cannot remember which flap comes next.", "A friend says, “I want the invitation to feel like a pocket, not a poster.” They ask whether you understand.", "A coworker says, “Your little notes are weirdly comforting,” and looks pleased that you made them.", "You are writing a grocery list, and you have already spelled “butter” three different ways without noticing.", "A laundromat machine finishes its cycle, but one sock is still caught in the rubber seal.", "You are watering a neglected office plant and notice that one leaf has a small brown square printed on it.", "A friend asks you to write a tiny sign for a community bookshelf. You can make it neat and easy to read, or add one odd detail that might make people smile and remember it.", "You are labeling a box of donated craft supplies. The label can be perfectly tidy, or you can keep a tiny missing corner visible so the box looks handled by a person.", "At a small workshop, someone offers you the microphone. You want to speak, but you are worried your voice will sound too much like the speaker you have been listening to all afternoon.", "During a quiet lunch, a coworker says, “Your little notes feel like a door left open. Do you ever worry they’re too strange?”", "A new volunteer says, “I’m nervous about welcoming people. I’m good with facts, not feelings.” She asks how you would introduce yourself.", "You have two pens for writing a short farewell note: a smooth ballpoint, or a fountain pen that skips when you slow down.", "Your sketchbook page is caught in the drawer’s lip. You need a sheet of paper, and pulling it free may tear the page.", "A stranger at a café tells you, “My favorite notes have one odd phrase tucked inside.” You are writing them a reply, and you have enough paper to add one.", "While adding a final line to a note, your pen leaks a dark circle over the last three words. You have only a minute before the person arrives.", "You are reading instructions aloud to a friend and lose your place halfway through the third step. They are waiting for you to continue.", "A stack of cards you gathered fans across the floor. One card lands ink-side up, and another slides under the table.", "You remember the smell of stale coffee and the back of a drawer, but not which note brought those two things together. You only have one loose sketchbook page to work from.", "A sentence keeps returning: “The sky stayed black too long.” You know someone said it, but not who, where, or what happened next.", "At the bottom of your bag, you find a scrap with three unfinished fragments: “second cup,” “door open,” and “still here.” You remember writing them, but nothing else.", "You are choosing a receipt to keep from a hardware store. Most are thin and dull; one has a pale blue stain in the corner.", "The corner of your desk drawer catches on a loose sketchbook page every time you close it. You could trim the page, but a few faint pencil marks sit near the edge.", "You are wiping a table after lunch and find three crumbs arranged beside the sugar packet: one, two, three.", "A balloon pops behind you while you are standing at a narrow counter.", "Someone drops a metal teaspoon onto a tiled floor three feet away, and it rings sharply.", "You lift a paper cup from a desk and notice the bottom is damp, leaving a cool ring on your fingertips.", "You are packing a handmade bookmark for a book club. You can print a neat title on it, or leave one word slightly faded so it looks like it was carried through several readings.", "You are washing a small paintbrush and notice that three bristles bend in different directions.", "While cutting a strip of paper for a gift tag, you accidentally cut your sleeve instead of the paper. The tag is still uncut.", "At a small reading, the host says, “I’m glad you came. The room feels warmer when you stay near the door.” You have been standing by the entrance.", "You remember placing a folded note beneath a saucer this morning. By lunch, you know it was there, but you cannot remember what you wrote or who it was for.", "A stack of plastic lids suddenly slides off the counter and lands with a sharp crackling sound.", "A friend asks you to read a short poem you wrote. You love one line, but you also suspect that reading aloud will make your voice sound too much like another poet in the room.", "You have to choose a pencil for marking a page in a borrowed book. One is blunt, and one leaves a faint line that catches on the paper.", "You are sorting paper clips by size, but one small clip keeps sliding under the tray.", "You are tracing a small label onto a parcel. The pen skips twice, and the second line ends in a blot where the final letter should be.", "Someone says, “Your notes are quiet, but they stay with me.” They look as if they might be waiting for you to explain.", "A coworker hands back a folder with a note tucked inside. You wrote it, but only the words “second cup” remain in your memory.", "A paperclip springs loose from a folder and lands against your wrist.", "A stranger asks you to describe your favorite kind of note. You want to give a clear answer, but you also want to hide how strange your real answer sounds.", "You are making a tiny sign for a community noticeboard. You can use block capitals, or tiny lowercase letters that follow the curve of the paper.", "A vending machine dispenses one wrapped mint instead of the two you expected.", "You are reading directions for a small bookshelf aloud to a friend. After the second step, you forget which piece goes next and cannot find your place on the page.", "You open a cupboard for a mug and find a small card tucked behind the cups. It says, “After the third bell,” but the bell means nothing to you now.", "A cold metal ruler is placed unexpectedly against the back of your neck.", "You are asked to sign a shared card with room for a joke. You have one good line, but it borrows the phrase the person beside you has been repeating all afternoon.", "A coworker asks you to write a short welcome note for a new volunteer. You can make it clear and practical, or leave one oddly reassuring sentence that might stick with her.", "You are making a birthday card for a friend who admires neat handwriting. You want to impress her with careful lettering, but you also want to add a tiny crooked cloud that only you would understand.", "At a small workshop, the host asks whether you would read your short poem aloud. You have memorized it, but you also want to borrow the host’s warm, measured rhythm so people will listen.", "A neighbor says, “Your little notes are strange, but they make me slow down.” They seem unsure whether that is a compliment.", "During a meeting, a colleague says, “Your turn. Try sounding like the person who just spoke.” You admire her clear, brisk phrasing but worry that copying it will make you sound false.", "You must label three small jars of craft supplies. One marker writes evenly, one dries with a faint shine, and one leaves a tiny gray cloud at the end of every line.", "Your sketchbook page is trapped in a drawer’s lip, but the loose sketchbook page inside it contains faint pencil marks. You need paper, and pulling harder may tear the page.", "You are making a tiny paper boat for a child. You can make the folds mathematically even, or leave one side slightly uneven so the boat rocks a little in the hand.", "While adding the last line to a handmade note, the pen leaks a dark circle over the final three words. The person who will read it arrives in one minute.", "You are giving directions for folding a small paper pocket. You know three steps clearly, but after the third you cannot remember whether the flap goes inside or outside.", "While reaching for a sheet of paper, your elbow knocks a mug of cold coffee across the desk. It stops against a stack of index cards, and a brown ring appears on the top card.", "This morning you tucked a blue paperclip beside your keys because a friend once said blue meant “keep the small door.” By noon, you remember the clip but not why it matters.", "A note in your pocket says, “After the third bell,” but you cannot remember what was supposed to happen then. You can see three faint pencil taps near the bottom.", "You remember placing a folded card at the back of your drawer yesterday. Today you remember the card, but not its color or what you wrote on it.", "You are writing a grocery list while making coffee. You have already written “buter” twice and are unsure whether the third spelling is any better.", "A vending machine drops one wrapped mint instead of the two you paid for. You have a receipt in your hand and no one else is waiting.", "At the laundromat, one sock remains caught in the rubber seal of a machine. You have a plastic bag for the rest of your laundry.", "A coworker presses a stapler with a sharp metallic snap directly beside your elbow.", "A desk fan suddenly changes speed while you are holding a stack of thin receipt papers. The airflow lifts the top three sheets.", "A cardboard box near your desk suddenly collapses inward with a soft crackle, sending a puff of dust across your sleeves."]

